method: every name, endpoint, seat, logging statement, filtering and policy read on its operator's own pages
  (sources.tsv); the logging and filtering columns summarise them in our own words. Addresses are the page's
  own where it lists them for the endpoint's host (Quad9, Cloudflare's one.one.one.one, AdGuard, DNS4EU);
  where it does not (Cloudflare's cloudflare-dns.com, Control D's freedns.controld.com and
  p0.freedns.controld.com), they are what two resolvers over DoH, certificates checked (dns.quad9.net and
  cloudflare-dns.com), both answered on 2026-11-20. Every endpoint answered one query (example.com A) over its
  own protocol at a bootstrap address, its certificate checked, on 2026-11-20.
audit: only Cloudflare publishes an examination of its claim (a Big 4 accounting firm, report of 2026-04-01 on
  its privacy commitments over 2024); none is named on the pages of Quad9, AdGuard, Control D or DNS4EU.
pins: none published by any operator, so '-' everywhere.
left-out: dns0.eu (closed in October 2025; on 2026-11-20 its domain had no address) and Mullvad's public
  encrypted DNS (closed on 2026-11-02, data/resolvers/SOURCE): closed services are never offered. NextDNS:
  its own pages document no endpoint for use without an account (each configuration has its own ID; the
  account-free endpoints appear only in its CLI's source), while its privacy page says nothing is logged
  unless the user asks for it. The other variants of these operators (Quad9's ECS service, AdGuard's default
  and family servers, Control D's filtered resolvers, DNS4EU's protective ones, 1.1.1.1 for Families): only the
  unfiltered service of each, and Quad9's default malware blocking, were chosen.
